<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tirukkural</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kural, Thirukkural</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tirukkural is a classic Tamil work of 1,330 couplets on ethics, attributed to the poet Thiruvalluvar. Divided into three books on virtue, wealth and love, it gives practical guidance on personal conduct, kingship and household life. Its date is debated, with many scholars placing it around the 5th century CE, and it is among the most widely read and translated works of Indian literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tirukkural.jpg" alt="Tirukkural" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: பேரா. சுந்தரசண்முகனார் · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%AE%A4%E0%AE%BF%E0%AE%B0%E0%AF%81%E0%AE%95%E0%AF%8D%E0%AE%95%E0%AF%81%E0%AE%B1%E0%AE%B3%E0%AF%8D_%E0%AE%A4%E0%AF%86%E0%AE%B3%E0%AE%BF%E0%AE%B5%E0%AF%81.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tirukkural, &quot;sacred couplets&quot;, is a classic Tamil text on commoner's morality, made of 1,330 short couplets of seven words each. The word kural means something short and concise, and the couplets are in the kural venba metre. Traditional accounts describe the work as the last of the third Sangam, put to a divine test that it passed, but linguistic analysis suggests a later date of 450 to 500 CE, after the Sangam period. Proposed dates range from 300 BCE to the 5th century CE. Kamil Zvelebil finds the earliest dates unacceptable, because nothing in the text supports them.</p><p>The author is traditionally Valluvar, or Thiruvalluvar, who is known by many other names. Almost nothing authentic is known about him, and the text names neither its author nor its original title. The name Thiruvalluvar first appears in a later Shaivite text, the Tiruvalluva Maalai, of unclear date, which says nothing of his birth, family or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>. The many legends about his life date from the early 19th century and are inconsistent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work has 133 chapters of ten couplets each. Book I, Aram, on virtue, covers chapters 1 to 38 and the moral values of an individual. Book II, Porul, on polity, covers chapters 39 to 108 and treats society and administration, with chapters on kings, ministers, taxes, justice, forts, war, agriculture, education and abstinence from alcohol. Book III, Inbam, on love, covers chapters 109 to 133 and deals with friendship, love, sexual union and domestic life. It stresses ahimsa, non-violence and moral vegetarianism, along with truthfulness, self-restraint, gratitude, hospitality and kindness.</p><p>Zvelebil holds that a single author wrote it, since its language, form and structure are consistent, and that it is not an anthology. The division into three books is probably the author's, while the subdivisions found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> are likely later. He describes its approach as pragmatic idealism, concerned with man in the totality of his relationships, and finds true poetry mainly in the third book. The text opens with an invocation of God and a praise of rain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The work is called the &quot;Tamil Veda&quot; and the &quot;Divine Book&quot;. It has influenced writers and leaders including Ilango Adigal, Kambar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-schweitzer">Albert Schweitzer</a>. It has been translated into at least 57 Indian and other languages and has not been out of print since it was first printed in 1812. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamil people</a> and the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> have long upheld it with reverence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kural” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much Tamil ethical literature, possibly including parts of the <b>Tirukkural</b> tradition, is often dated to this period.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalabhra-dynasty">Kalabhra dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
